\section{Introduction}
\label{introduction}


\section{Related works}

\section{Materials and methods}

\subsection{Evaluation}

Unlike classical Machine Learning problems, such as classification or regression, evaluating generative artificial intelligence models poses a genuine challenge. This is due to the infinite space of correct \textit{outputs} (responses). The approach in \cite{mi-paper} was to use the \textit{LLM-as-a-judge} method, a very common solution in the state of the art given its simplicity: it consists of having another language model evaluate the responses. However, this type of evaluation is highly vulnerable to technical hallucinations, suffers from self-reference bias, and tends to lose consistency.

Therefore, \textbf{an experimental evaluation methodology has been designed}, which is detailed in this section. This approach, designed to optimize the consumption of operational resources while maintaining statistical robustness, consists of two main stages: evaluation of the complete system and analysis of marginal contributions through an ablation study.

The main challenge of this experimental methodology, and what differentiates it from other existing approaches, is that it must be able to address the evaluation of geospatial context usage in response generation, not merely the quality of the responses themselves. A response that is correct but generic, or even seemingly specific but generated from the model's base knowledge, is not equivalent to a \textit{personalized} response adapted to terrain characteristics. Therefore, multiple stages are combined, different configurations are evaluated, and specific metrics are proposed.

\subsection{Parcel Selection and Heterogeneity}

A total of 15 parcels were selected, categorized into four different Land Use (LU) types. The distribution of evaluations is weighted according to the operational utility of the tool for each functional land use, as shown in \tabref{tab:land_use}.

To ensure an unbiased evaluation, parcels were selected to represent a high degree of heterogeneity in their physical characteristics, including variable slopes ($0.5\%$ to $21.6\%$), altitudes, irregular shapes, and diversity in vegetation health (NDVI ranges). The parcels are shown in \figref{fig:parcel_mosaic}.


